<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    WPR.Vibration.AndroidVibrator - IVibrationProvider over android.os.Vibrator.

    A MODULE: it implements a contract owned by WPR.Engine.Vibration and is plugged in by a head
    declaring caps.Vibration(...). One seam, one technology, one project reference - the shape
    WPR.Input.XamarinEssentials has.

    Named for the TECHNOLOGY, not the platform, per the naming note in CLAUDE.md. "AndroidVibrator"
    is the API (android.os.Vibrator / VibratorManager), the same way AndroidChannel is the
    notification-channel API; a future controller-rumble sibling filling the same seam would be
    WPR.Vibration.Gamepad and would name no platform at all.

    Android-only, so the TFM is singular <TargetFramework>: Src/Directory.Build.targets only strips
    android legs from a plural <TargetFrameworks>, so the singular form is what keeps this intact on
    a machine without the workload, and WPR.Windows.slnf is the mechanism that keeps it out of a
    desktop build.

    NOTE the head must declare android.permission.VIBRATE. It is a normal (install-time) permission,
    so there is no runtime request to make - but without it every call throws SecurityException,
    which this logs as [wpr-vibrate] and swallows.

    No package reference: Vibrator, VibratorManager and VibrationEffect are all in Mono.Android.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-android</TargetFramework>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>21.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>
    <AssemblyName>WPR.Vibration.AndroidVibrator</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>WPR.Vibration.AndroidVibrator</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Engine\WPR.Engine.Vibration\WPR.Engine.Vibration.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
